Here's a C++ function that takes a reference to a `std::string` containing markdown formatted text and returns a `std::string` containing HTML formatted text:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <sstream>
#include <regex>

std::string markdownToHtml(const std::string& markdown) {
    std::string html;
    std::stringstream ss(markdown);
    std::string line;
    while (std::getline(ss, line)) {
        // Convert markdown to HTML using regex
        std::regex re(R"(^>\s*([^\n]*)<(\s*$)");
        std::string match;
        if (std::regex_search(line, match, re)) {
            html += "<p>" + match[1] + "</p>";
        } else {
            html += "<p>" + line + "</p>";
        }
    }
    return html;
}
```

This function uses a regular expression to match markdown headers and convert them to HTML paragraphs. The regular expression `^>\s*([^\n]*)<(\s*$)` matches lines that start with `>` and end with `</`, which are markdown headers. The function then wraps the matched text in a `<p>` tag, or wraps the entire line in a `<p>` tag.

